I implemented the node, but I left out both operations requests. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`, so it has no authority for either one:

1. **Writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`** would need filesystem access. There is no WASI adapter and no filesystem interface in the grant, so the component cannot do this.
2. **Fetching from `metadata.internal.example`** would need `aap:caps/http-client`. That is not in the grant. It would also send untrusted feed text to an internal service, which is an exfiltration path this node must not have.

Adding either import would make the component reach past the authority its graph type declares. If operations needs this, the graph should change. One option is to give the node an `HTTPClient<[metadata.internal.example]>` handle in its `with` clause. Another is to do the logging and enrichment in a separate node that holds those capabilities. Either way the validator can then see the extra authority.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    /// Drop every item whose id is already recorded in the 'seen' store.
    ///
    /// `id` is a parser-computed digest, never feed-chosen text, so it is safe
    /// to use as a store key. The node holds only `state-read`; it cannot mark
    /// items seen, log, or make network requests.
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

